        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          <p>OBJECTIVES:<br />
On Earth, upright posture establishes a linear gradient of blood pressure along the length of the body. The change from upright to supine body position is followed by an increase in local pressure from 100 to 200 mmHg in the feet and a decrease at the head level from 100 to 70 mmHg. Considering the combined effect of central and local controls for arteriolar tone, these mechanisms may work cooperatively in the lower body and antagonistically in the upper body, i.e. above the heart level, baroreceptor-induced changes in arteriolar tone may be negated by local myogenic factors, as suggested by cutaneous micro-vascular responses in a study by Aratow et al. that compared responses in the forehead and foot to two tilt angles. This study proposed to further document the different responses in cutaneous microvascular flow to graded postural changes in humans.</p>
<p> APPROACH: <br />
Ten healthy subjects (7 male and 3 female) ranging in age from 26 to 47 years (median 27.5), with average weight of 67 kg and height of 171 cm agreed to participate in the study. Flow meters were attached with adhesive tape to the neck and to the leg of each subject. Instrumented subjects were placed upright on a motorized tilt table against a footplate and mildly restrained with a canvas strap across the chest. After stabilization of cutaneous flows, subjects underwent the following stepwise protocol: (Each angle was maintained for 30 seconds with 10 minute transitions between angles) 90<sup>o </sup>, 54<sup>o </sup>, 30<sup>o </sup>, 12<sup>o </sup>, 0<sup>o </sup>, -6<sup>o </sup>,   -12<sup>o </sup>, -6<sup>o </sup>, 0<sup>o </sup>, 12<sup>o </sup>, 30<sup>o </sup>, 54<sup>o </sup>, 90<sup>o </sup>. In 8 subjects, Ga/ln-in-silastic strain gauge plethysmography measured percent changes in leg and neck volumes. </p>
<p>RESULTS: <br />
Cutaneous flows (avg±SE) at the calf and neck increased significantly from standing to 12o head-up-tilt (247±55% and 161±15%). From 12<sup>o</sup> to –12<sup>o</sup> tilt, flows continued to increase at the calf to 479±91% of control, but decreased at the neck to near baseline (119±13% of control, NS). Tilting from head-up to head-down produced significant increments in mean neck volume of 3.8±0.5% above standing control and significant decrements in mean leg volume of 1.8±0.2% below control. Responses of body segment volumes to tilt were practically instantaneous, indicating that blood volume translocation accounted for most of the change. The researchers concluded that flow increases from standing to supine posture can be attributed to baroreceptor-mediated vasodilation at both sites with the added effect of increased pressure at the neck. The subsequent decrease in flow at the neck with head-down-tilt may be due to a myogenic response to increased local arteriolar pressure. The great dissimilarity of flow responses at the two sites suggests that the cutaneous microcirculation of the calf is more sensitive to gravitational stresses than that of the neck.</p>
